{
  "analysis": "Tools are now installed: ffmpeg, ffprobe, tesseract, and convert. The video is 1280x720, 30fps, 190.1 seconds long (~5703 frames). We need to extract frames and OCR the Zork terminal text to identify player input moves. First, we should sample a few frames to understand the visual layout and OCR quality.",
  "plan": "Create a frames directory and extract several sample frames at different timestamps (e.g., 1s, 30s, 60s, 120s, 180s). Then run tesseract OCR on them to see what text is recognizable and determine the terminal layout, text colors, and how to distinguish player input from game output.",
  "commands": [
    {
      "keystrokes": "mkdir -p /app/frames /app/ocr\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "for t in 1 5 15 30 60 90 120 150 180; do ffmpeg -y -ss $t -i /app/video.mp4 -frames:v 1 /app/frames/sample_${t}.png >/dev/null 2>&1; done\n",
      "duration": 2.0
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "ls -lh /app/frames\n",
      "duration": 0.1
    },
    {
      "keystrokes": "for t in 1 5 15 30 60 90 120 150 180; do echo \"===== $t =====\"; tesseract /app/frames/sample_${t}.png stdout 2>/dev/null; done\n",
      "duration": 5.0
    }
  ],
  "task_complete": false
}
